.TH METRICFS 4
.SH NAME
metricfs \- the machine's own numbers as a file tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount {metricfs} /n/metric
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Metricfs
serves one file per measurement, taken from files the kernel already
keeps.  There is no metrics protocol here, no scrape endpoint and no
exposition format: a monitor reads files, and because a namespace can
be exported it reads them on another machine with the same code.
.IP
.EX
# on the machine being watched
mount {metricfs} /n/metric
styxlisten -A 'tcp!*!6666' export /n/metric

# anywhere else
mount -A tcp!host!6666 /n/host
cat /n/host/mem/main/used
.EE
.SH FILES
Every leaf holds one number and a newline, except
.B osversion
and
.B sysname
which hold a string, and
.B log
which holds text.
.TP
.B uptime
Seconds since this machine started.
.TP
.B time
Seconds since the epoch.
.TP
.B procs
How many processes exist.
.TP
.B osversion
The kernel's build date, as
.IR cons (3)
reports it.
.TP
.B sysname
The machine's name.
.TP
.B log
The kernel message buffer,
.BR /dev/kmesg .
.TP
.BI cpu/ n /
One directory per processor, named by its number, holding
.BR cs ,
.BR intr ,
.BR syscall ,
.BR pfault ,
.BR tlbfault ,
.BR tlbpurge ,
.B load
and
.BR idle .
These are the columns of
.B /dev/sysstat
in order, named, so that a reader does not have to know which column is
which.
.TP
.BI mem/ pool /
One directory per memory pool, named by the pool, holding
.BR used ,
.BR max ,
.BR highwater ,
.BR nalloc ,
.BR nfree ,
.B nbrk
and
.BR largest .
These are the columns of
.BR /dev/memory .
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/metricfs.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cons (3),
.IR styxlisten (1),
.IR export (4),
.IR supervise (1)
.SH "A FILE IS OFFERED ONLY IF IT CAN BE MEASURED"
A leaf exists only when its source can be read, so the tree has fewer
files on some machines than others.  A metrics tree that answers 0 for
something it cannot measure is worse than one that does not offer the
file, because the zero is indistinguishable from a real reading.
.PP
Under hosted
.IR emu (1)
there is no
.BR /dev/sysstat ,
no
.B /dev/osversion
and no
.BR /dev/kmesg ,
so
.BR cpu/ ,
.B osversion
and
.B log
are absent.  There is no uptime either:
.I emu
serves
.B /dev/msec
but it reads 0 forever, and
.B /dev/time
there is a single microsecond count of the wall clock, which says
nothing about when the machine started.  The source is chosen by its
value and not merely by whether the file opens.
.SH BUGS
The shape of the tree is fixed when
.I metricfs
starts, from the processors and pools the machine has then.  A
processor started later does not appear until it is restarted.
.PP
Nothing is cached and nothing is sampled: a file has the value it had
when the read happened.  Reading twenty files is twenty reads of the
underlying source.
.PP
The column names promise something about the kernel.  If
.BR devcons.c 's
.B sysstat
loop or
.BR alloc.c 's
.B poolread
changes its order, the names here have to change with it.
